Falsifies `machine_discovery::framebuffer::verification::an_accepted_span_covers_every_pixel_the_geometry_describes`.

The defect: the "is the stride at least one row wide" guard multiplies the width by
four without saturating.

Why this is the plausible spelling: `self.stride < self.width * 4` is what the
sentence in the doc says, and the `saturating_mul` reads like defensive noise
around it. It is not. A width above 2^30 wraps the product to something *small*,
so the guard passes on geometry it exists to refuse, and `span` then returns
`stride * height` bytes for a screen whose rows are wider than the stride. The
console paints inside that span and shears off the end of it.

The geometry comes out of a boot handoff, and `span` is documented as "the only
validation a description read out of a boot handoff can be given". Nothing else
looks at it.

Expected to fail: the arithmetic-overflow check on `self.width * 4`, and behind it
`assert!(span as u64 >= pixels)`.

